WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.668
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-winning writer,

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:04.503
director, and producer.
His latest movie,

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:06.840
"Please Don't Destroy:
The Treasure of Foggy Mountain,"

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.242
is streaming exclusively
on Peacock November 17th.

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:10.576
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:12.578
-I think I know where the
treasure of Foggy Mountain is,

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:14.247
and I think
we should go get it.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:15.915
-The treasure of Foggy Mountain?

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:17.884
Like, from when we were kids?

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:20.052
-It all started
when I saw a TikTok

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:21.454
about this guy --

00:00:21.454 --> 00:00:23.623
Deetch Nordwind.

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.625
-Whoa!

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.560
You have like 40 tabs open.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.895
What are you doing, man?

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:30.863
-Well, that's how much
research I've been doing.

00:00:30.863 --> 00:00:33.333
-Well, a lot of these are clips
from "Jennifer's Body."

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.134
Was that part of the research?
-Um...

00:00:35.134 --> 00:00:36.736
-You could just rent the movie
at that point.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:38.237
-Yeah.
-Enough.

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:39.305
-Please welcome
back to the show

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:41.074
our friend Judd Apatow,
everybody!

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:43.142
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:52.519 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:54.821
Welcome back!
-Yes!

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:56.388
-I want to get to the movie,

00:00:56.388 --> 00:00:57.890
but I also, I want
to start talking

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.893
about your very talented
daughter, Maude.

00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:01.093
-Yes.

00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:03.496
-Who was recently doing
"Cabaret" in London.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.498
-That's right.
-Onstage in London.

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:06.665
-Look at that.
-That's thrilling.

00:01:06.665 --> 00:01:08.334
That must be thrilling
as a father.

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.169
-Oh, my God, it's so exciting.
I went 12 times.

00:01:10.169 --> 00:01:11.771
-Did you really go 12 times?
-I know.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:14.040
The cast sometimes
would look at me out there

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.241
like, "Again?"
-Yeah.

00:01:16.241 --> 00:01:19.245
So, tell me what it's like
as a father. Is it just...

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:22.248
Does it just fly by when you get
to watch a child onstage?

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:23.482
I mean, seeing it 12 times,

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:25.651
does it just feel amazing
every time?

00:01:25.651 --> 00:01:28.921
-Uh, I'm so nervous
the first three or four times.

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:30.856
-Yeah.
-And then I can enjoy it.

00:01:30.856 --> 00:01:32.691
And then by the end of the run,

00:01:32.691 --> 00:01:35.695
I feel like it's some
major accomplishment of mine.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:37.196
-Yes.
-You know?

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:39.399
So it's, like, really fun
'cause I don't have to do it,

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:41.167
but when it's over,
I'm like, "We did it."

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:43.502
[ Laughter ]

00:01:43.502 --> 00:01:45.504
-Now, one of the reasons
you're here promoting the movie

00:01:45.504 --> 00:01:47.140
is because of the SAG strike.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.474
-There is a strike.
I don't know if you know.

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:50.309
-There is a strike.
-It's happening right now.

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:51.977
-Yes, and yes.

00:01:51.977 --> 00:01:53.546
-It's a big strike.
-It's a big strike.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:55.915
And because of that,
the guys in the movie,

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.184
because they're SAG actors,
cannot promote the film.

00:01:58.184 --> 00:02:00.920
You are a producer of the film,
so you can.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.423
But because you can,
and only you can...

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:06.592
-I'm the only person
affiliated with the movie

00:02:06.592 --> 00:02:08.328
who's allowed to talk
about the movie.

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:10.262
-Yeah.
-Everyone else has to pretend

00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:12.098
it does not exist.
-Yeah.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:13.432
And in fact, the three,

00:02:13.432 --> 00:02:15.201
the Please Don't Destroy guys
will be here tomorrow,

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:16.702
and we're not gonna be able
to talk about it.

00:02:16.702 --> 00:02:18.471
-You'd better not!
-We won't.

00:02:18.471 --> 00:02:20.106
-I don't want you to mention.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:22.108
-It'll be weird 'cause
I'm talking to you about it now

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.342
and they're gonna be here
tomorrow,

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:24.376
and they're gonna be like,
"Why is he not even

00:02:24.376 --> 00:02:25.878
bringing it up?"
-Yeah.

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:28.214
-But because you're the only one
who can talk about it,

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:30.316
it has affected things like
the press at the premiere.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:33.152
-Yeah, that's who showed up.
-Like...

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:35.788
-Just one guy who looks like
Evan Goldberg showed up.

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.123
That's it.

00:02:37.123 --> 00:02:40.326
I mean, literally,
that's the entire press line.

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.394
And that's with me
standing there going,

00:02:42.394 --> 00:02:44.063
"Please take my picture."

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:46.032
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:48.033
-So, this is
one of the cool things.

00:02:48.033 --> 00:02:50.503
The Please Don't Destroy guys,
who are wonderful --

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:53.038
two of them, John Higgins
and Martin Herlihy,

00:02:53.038 --> 00:02:54.373
you know their dads.
-That's right.

00:02:54.373 --> 00:02:55.508
-You've known their dads
forever.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:57.376
-Yes.
John's dad is Steve Higgins,

00:02:57.376 --> 00:02:58.878
the great producer
from "Saturday Night Live,"

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:00.280
from "The Tonight Show."

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.381
And Martin's dad is Tim Herlihy,

00:03:02.381 --> 00:03:05.184
who wrote "The Wedding Singer"
and "Happy Gilmore."

00:03:05.184 --> 00:03:07.586
-A famous SNL writer, as well.
-So it's very funny,

00:03:07.586 --> 00:03:09.355
like, working with their kids

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:11.057
because they're young.
-Yeah.

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:13.226
-I am no longer young.
-Yeah.

00:03:13.226 --> 00:03:14.727
-And we would be sitting
in the woods

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:17.630
having these conversations,
and I would have flashbacks

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:20.666
of having the same conversations
with Adam Sandler and Tim,

00:03:20.666 --> 00:03:25.504
like, "Hm, I've had
this boner conversation before.

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:27.674
I've debated this joke before."

00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:30.576
-No, I will say,
I would not want to ever talk

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:33.746
to one of my dad's friends
about boners.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:35.581
But if I had to,

00:03:35.581 --> 00:03:37.249
I would think, like, Judd Apatow
would be the one.

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:38.651
Do they, like...?
-I would think that.

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.019
-Yeah.
-I would think that too.

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:42.355
But no matter what happens,
you're the creepy old guy.

00:03:42.355 --> 00:03:43.756
-Yeah.
-You know? 'Cause, like...

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.491
-I also want to note, I know
that you filmed it in the woods,

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:46.992
but it was weird
that you started that by,

00:03:46.992 --> 00:03:48.861
like, "We'd be in the woods
talking about it."

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:50.062
That didn't help.
-Well, no, no, no.

00:03:50.062 --> 00:03:51.363
I would lure them
into the woods.

00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:52.532
-You would lure them.

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:53.966
[ Laughter ]

00:03:53.966 --> 00:03:55.535
I do want to talk about

00:03:55.535 --> 00:03:57.369
maybe the fact
that you were having flashbacks.

00:03:57.369 --> 00:03:58.704
These are some
of your early days.

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:00.206
-Well, this is when
I was their age.

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:02.308
-So, this is crazy.
This is when you were their age.

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.076
These are some of the people
you were hanging out with.

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:05.644
-Yeah. That's right.
-So, that's you.

00:04:05.644 --> 00:04:07.146
And what do you got there?

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:10.149
-That's Shandling
and Springsteen at the Grammys.

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.250
-Wow.
-That's at the Grammys.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.885
-And you were writing
at the Grammys?

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:15.554
-That's my first job ever,
writing the Grammys.

00:04:15.554 --> 00:04:18.424
Obviously, I thought
I was kind of a cool

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:21.093
Sylvester Stallone type
at the time.

00:04:21.093 --> 00:04:22.294
-And got away with it.

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:23.696
Not bad
-A little bit.

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:25.965
This is -- Was this writing
for "The Ben Stiller Show"?

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:27.132
-Yes, that's
"The Ben Stiller Show."

00:04:27.132 --> 00:04:28.634
-So, you got Ben Stiller.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.570
-And now you know
why I have a beard.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:33.939
Because I look weird.
-Yeah. There you go.

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:35.274
-This is...
I mean, this is...

00:04:35.274 --> 00:04:37.076
Look at this A-list crew
right here.

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:39.344
So, and this is on the set
of "The Larry Sanders Show."

00:04:39.344 --> 00:04:41.347
-It's "The Larry Sanders Show."
And so that's with Gary,

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:43.916
and that's Warren Beatty,
and Rip Torn, and me, young.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:45.418
But here's the thing.

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:50.089
You know, I am them there.
Right?

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:52.858
Like, so that's like
the Please Don't Destroy guys,

00:04:52.858 --> 00:04:54.293
and I'm Rip Torn now.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.293 --> 00:04:55.661
-Right?

00:04:55.661 --> 00:04:57.463
-But you liked,
and I know from...

00:04:57.463 --> 00:04:59.098
Because you were always,
as a kid,

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:00.966
fascinated with comedy.
You went out of your way

00:05:00.966 --> 00:05:02.635
as a kid
to interview older comedians.

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:04.036
-Yes.
-So it must be weird.

00:05:04.036 --> 00:05:05.872
I know you have a lot of respect
for younger comedians,

00:05:05.872 --> 00:05:06.939
but are you still out there

00:05:06.939 --> 00:05:08.374
trying to talk
to older comedians?

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:10.643
-Well, now I have
to make older friends

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:13.479
so I can still be the young guy.
-Yeah.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.347
-That's my new thing.
-Wait, let me see.

00:05:15.347 --> 00:05:17.483
Because I want to make sure
we have one. This is...

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.451
-So, now, in order
to be the young guy,

00:05:19.451 --> 00:05:20.953
I have to hang out
with Mel Brooks.

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:22.020
-Yeah.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.657
-And Dick Van Dyke
and Norman Lear.

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.659
That's 300 years of comedy
right there.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:29.228
-They're literally
300 years old.

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:33.165
That's the only way you can
feel like John Higgins today

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:35.267
is to hang out with
300-year-old men.

00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:36.602
-Yeah, and talk about...

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:38.771
And talk about World War II
for hours and hours.

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:41.941
-I bet you talk about boners
a little bit.

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:44.443
-But they're still
all way funnier than us.

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:45.744
-Yes.
-Like, not even close.

00:05:45.744 --> 00:05:47.813
-It's really amazing.
I mean, it does...

00:05:47.813 --> 00:05:50.516
You know, I've been lucky enough
to meet a couple of them,

00:05:50.516 --> 00:05:53.486
and it's crazy how good they
still are at telling stories.

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:56.588
-Oh, yeah. Me and Bill Hader
went to visit Mel Brooks once,

00:05:56.588 --> 00:05:59.091
and when we left, he said,
"Come, come visit again.

00:05:59.091 --> 00:06:00.927
But not soon."

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:02.595
[ Laughter ]

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:04.864
"Like four months.
Wait four months."

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:06.365
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:08.834
And this was my group of, like,
Please Don't Destroy.

00:06:08.834 --> 00:06:12.838
This is us when we were young.
-And you've been opening

00:06:12.838 --> 00:06:14.907
for Adam Sandler recently
on the road.

00:06:14.907 --> 00:06:17.676
He's doing a standup tour,
and you've been opening for him

00:06:17.676 --> 00:06:19.144
for 40 years.
Is that true?

00:06:19.144 --> 00:06:21.013
-Yeah, I mean, I literally
started doing standup...

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:23.349
It'll be -- In a year
and a half, it'll be 40 years.

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:25.518
-Wow.
-Since the first time.

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:27.787
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:27.787 --> 00:06:30.490
I always think that anything
that's like 40 years earlier...

00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:32.358
I always think like,
if this was the '70s,

00:06:32.358 --> 00:06:34.193
I'd be talking
about W.C. Fields.

00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:36.162
-Yeah.
-Like, it's too much.

00:06:36.162 --> 00:06:37.663
And the sad part is,
it's been 40 years

00:06:37.663 --> 00:06:40.332
and I'm still opening for Adam.
You know?

00:06:40.332 --> 00:06:42.267
It never, like, reversed.
-It never flipped.

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:44.403
You never got anybody
who would open for you.

00:06:44.403 --> 00:06:45.905
-No, that's not possible.

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:48.074
But I lately have been thinking
I should stop doing standup,

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:49.342
and I've been, like,
kind of making a list,

00:06:49.342 --> 00:06:52.177
like pros and cons of,
like, should I quit?

00:06:52.177 --> 00:06:53.545
-Oh, that's interesting.

00:06:53.545 --> 00:06:55.982
And so these are the reasons
why I should quit.

00:06:55.982 --> 00:06:59.318
-Should?
-Yes.

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:03.523
"Too bad a memory to remember
jokes; must read off paper."

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:05.191
-Yeah.

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:06.692
-"Nothing to say."

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:07.960
-Nothing to say.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:10.162
-"Too rich to bother
writing jokes."

00:07:10.162 --> 00:07:12.665
-Yeah.
That'll get in the way.

00:07:12.665 --> 00:07:15.501
-"Don't know who Doja Cat is."
-Yeah.

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:17.303
Who -- Who does?

00:07:17.303 --> 00:07:19.805
-"No endgame."
-[ Laughs ]

00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:23.208
-"Hard to do with sciatica."
-Yeah.

00:07:23.208 --> 00:07:26.212
-"All my references
are from the 1900s."

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:27.546
-Yeah.

00:07:27.546 --> 00:07:30.483
-"The verdict is in --
I'm not charismatic."

00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:33.319
-Oh, well, I would wait that out
a little bit, yeah.

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:34.486
-The last one is,

00:07:34.486 --> 00:07:36.756
"Sick of riding in buses
with Rob Schneider."

00:07:36.756 --> 00:07:37.756
-Yeah,

00:07:37.756 --> 00:07:39.125
-Which is actually
kind of fun.

00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:40.793
-This is a crazy photo,

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:44.029
because this is
the 1992 "Young Comedians."

00:07:44.029 --> 00:07:46.365
-That's right.
-And I mean, I...

00:07:46.365 --> 00:07:49.301
Everybody here had a career.

00:07:49.301 --> 00:07:51.303
It's a really impressive
group of people.

00:07:51.303 --> 00:07:53.405
-That's right. Yeah.
And look at Carvey.

00:07:53.405 --> 00:07:55.874
Carvey looks the same.
-Yeah.

00:07:55.874 --> 00:07:57.543
-Look at me.
-Yeah.

00:07:57.543 --> 00:07:58.877
-The difference.

00:07:58.877 --> 00:08:01.947
-It is haunting
how much different you look

00:08:01.947 --> 00:08:04.283
and how much the same he looks.
-Yeah.

00:08:04.283 --> 00:08:06.585
This is the thing.
Here's my other list.

00:08:06.585 --> 00:08:08.053
Ready?
-You have two lists.

00:08:08.053 --> 00:08:10.089
-My second list, which I can't
remember, which is, you know,

00:08:10.089 --> 00:08:11.691
you have to stop doing standup
at a certain age

00:08:11.691 --> 00:08:13.925
'cause all your jokes
become about decay.

00:08:13.925 --> 00:08:15.461
-Yeah.
-Right?

00:08:15.461 --> 00:08:19.065
Because you -- that's all
you know in life, is decay.

00:08:19.065 --> 00:08:21.433
And so all your jokes
are kind of like,

00:08:21.433 --> 00:08:23.569
"Hey, isn't diverticulitis
funny?"

00:08:23.569 --> 00:08:25.237
-Yeah.

00:08:25.237 --> 00:08:26.405
-"Don't you hate it

00:08:26.405 --> 00:08:27.906
when all your girlfriends
from high school

00:08:27.906 --> 00:08:30.076
start dying of natural causes?"

00:08:30.076 --> 00:08:31.877
[ Laughter ]

00:08:31.877 --> 00:08:34.646
-That's a good one.
I like that one.

00:08:34.646 --> 00:08:36.148
I mean, I don't know
if it's a closer,

00:08:36.148 --> 00:08:38.150
'cause everybody's
super bummed out, but...

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:39.518
[ Both laugh ]
-It's both.

00:08:39.518 --> 00:08:40.952
-It is a delight
to have you here.

00:08:40.952 --> 00:08:43.422
Congrats on the movie.
That's Judd Apatow, everybody.

00:08:43.422 --> 00:08:45.657
-"Please Don't Destroy:
The Treasure of Foggy Mountain"

00:08:45.657 --> 00:08:48.327
begins streaming exclusively
on Peacock November 17th.

00:08:48.327 --> 00:08:50.396
We'll be right back with music
from Rett Madison.

